#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198015 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd546dda-c78f-5367-b03e-9b3acb420040" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "e8d75369-b381-53d3-b3af-37c6109c15a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12327" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{y-x}{x+y} \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\: 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4 \\right\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n![](images/99/99eef26d2de27b8ae6f8cf872b352368efaf2703e47bcea6e406478db14d6e18.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\qquad 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = y-x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n![](images/e7/e7d00c49df06196f76846eeee5152f258a53c4936b51285233531872906ced6b.svg)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-x}{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{u}{v}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198016 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2682751-23e4-5cf2-9a46-82c75765423f" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Integrand"}] :question/id #uuid "1ad4ddff-b776-50d9-b698-c0172ee9d3ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-12328" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_D (2x+y)y^2 \\:\\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $\\left(\\dfrac32,0\\right) ,$ $\\left(\\dfrac52,0\\right) ,$ $(1,3),$ and $(0,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/3d/3d526a4897f071f523b5900c55fd8797cf5e4270a3031270013e54dc9dca9ca4.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  (2x+y)y^2\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\n\\qquad\nv=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/ae/aeb4cdc398b9f13551ecea013fef9ea607786ab1f9d3466da846dd10b930f378.svg)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(5,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2\\cdot 1+3,3) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(0,3) & =(2\\cdot 0+3,3) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 5, \\quad 0 \\leq v \\leq 3 \\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/c2/c296b1ee0451686608ec4b06c29d114ff1918233886d8471d3b2c118851aa25e.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y){y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198235 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed8ac2-8d21-5bc9-b1cc-df20760cfbfb" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "9d477733-7178-5b88-926f-a457faaf344a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7285" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq 4x+2y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 3 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u = 4x+2y$ and $v=y - x,$ write the following integral in terms of $u$ and $v.$ \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iint\\limits_D \\sin \\left(3(x+y)\\right)  \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/e9/e9a07b75be73621bb23edb6f4da67999c5f5ce07b8776f549cf12464e74fa702.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm d x \\, \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v))  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\mathrm d u \\, \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = 4x + 2y, \\qquad v = y - x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/a1/a1228e77194b92d8b2c527845273ae9f70d9b90d4c3091f5ed9dd9f8c430776c.svg)\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables.\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu = 4x+2y, \\qquad v = y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{u-2v}6, \\qquad y =\\dfrac{u+4v}6.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(x+y))\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{u-2v}{6}+\\frac{u+4v}{6}))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{2u+2v}{6}))|\\frac{1}{6}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}sin\\,(u+v)\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed8ac2-8d21-5bc9-b1cc-df20760cfbfb" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6364af2a-3d6b-5851-9049-3595e8cfce71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480922} {:answer/id #uuid "8236c364-9a58-5bd5-bca8-e007796586fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480987} {:answer/id #uuid "91e046c8-5b65-5972-9eef-cd8f897a8cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481012} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc70cb2-b436-5aca-b9d7-fabe9b0e5de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481154} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8d0347-5303-50ad-8ea6-72f1ba59d8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc8d0347-5303-50ad-8ea6-72f1ba59d8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481229} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b345d81-65d6-5a3f-9150-c97ad1b7b016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea750ce2-b7b3-5ba2-80fa-7d5a87470671" :question/math-academy-id "q-160850" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{3/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{3/2}|-\\frac{1}{2}|du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}]dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{2}{5}\\,{v}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed8ac2-8d21-5bc9-b1cc-df20760cfbfb" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6b5fe5-29d1-5793-a274-fa3b5699e913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({e}^{-1/2}-{e}^{-2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493410} {:answer/id #uuid "17dcff5e-6574-5e2e-be4c-22c67c1a31d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493432} {:answer/id #uuid "702b0bc2-ac10-5437-9f18-9fa877532a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493554} {:answer/id #uuid "857734df-e10d-5568-a049-4e87b8b5c0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493601} {:answer/id #uuid "e162164d-9035-55fa-9f1c-b47ea0bae241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{2}-e)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b6b5fe5-29d1-5793-a274-fa3b5699e913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({e}^{-1/2}-{e}^{-2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493410} :answer-field/id #uuid "15ea503e-c96f-567a-8f89-cf26cc3e1193" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5c02db8-6f5e-5ac6-a24e-9e9016b1082e" :question/math-academy-id "q-160899" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{x}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{-xy/2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{x}{4}\\le y\\le 2x,\\,1\\le xy\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2y}{x})}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{-xy/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{-v/2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1/4}^{2}]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{4}]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\cdot {[-2{e}^{-v/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot ({e}^{-2}-{e}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{-1/2}-{e}^{-2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed8ac2-8d21-5bc9-b1cc-df20760cfbfb" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f86c71-5ba2-5262-9bbf-7f7163efa286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9({e}^{4}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493398} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6403cc-10a5-5873-b7f2-714dc7812c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9({e}^{3}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493498} {:answer/id #uuid "c84e1d3d-5e71-590c-befa-918bc686cd64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(1-{e}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493712} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cbdccf-0d8d-5a1e-bca5-5206ced248f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5({e}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493730} {:answer/id #uuid "f587b155-dbaf-5156-98a6-ca8c0ee16997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10({e}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04f86c71-5ba2-5262-9bbf-7f7163efa286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9({e}^{4}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493398} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ad75dc-fe53-5e66-9950-00b2a3fe62bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1c41fe9-56fa-5a4c-8bab-8d810d33bac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-38371" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=xy$ and $v=y+x,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le y+x\\le 10\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=y+x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 10\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n.\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{y-x}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,\\frac{1}{|y-x|}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}{e}^{u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[v]_{1}^{10}\\,du \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =9[{e}^{u}]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =9({e}^{4}-e).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed8ac2-8d21-5bc9-b1cc-df20760cfbfb" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0881d141-176a-5207-829e-e7fcf9748ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(v-u)^{4}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375497} {:answer/id #uuid "72802f78-8fa2-5be5-8cb9-94054dadb960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375498} {:answer/id #uuid "9d50078b-ccc0-5312-be32-aa00de941511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(v-u)^{2}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375499} {:answer/id #uuid "ce22a068-82fa-5eda-a3a4-b436e880bc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375500} {:answer/id #uuid "f22feb1c-862a-595f-aaba-980169e06dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(v-u)^{2}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72802f78-8fa2-5be5-8cb9-94054dadb960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375498} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b052ab4-b874-5259-8af3-5e39e362471e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff0203f2-b0b4-5368-8eaf-f5cdf929fd73" :question/math-academy-id "q-82808" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y-x\\le 1,\\,0\\le x+y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=y-x$ and $v=x+y,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}8xy\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-x,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=y-x,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v-u}{2},\\,y=\\frac{v+u}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}8(\\frac{v-u}{2})(\\frac{v+u}{2})|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed8ac2-8d21-5bc9-b1cc-df20760cfbfb" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143bd49e-b2ca-5b56-9eca-6d7751d7c1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493428} {:answer/id #uuid "37392f66-38d1-5cf1-a83a-b61ffbecdc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493480} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9d745e-3b7a-5566-acd4-df51a55a0434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493492} {:answer/id #uuid "757701d0-e96c-58b7-9b6d-52ab1067ccc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493565} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b8b11d-0fcd-5a7a-8d95-67c9db317553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "143bd49e-b2ca-5b56-9eca-6d7751d7c1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493428} :answer-field/id #uuid "b260735c-8ce2-5820-82bd-e9db5e7533a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9c37ee3-304c-5283-97a5-75391f3d1e94" :question/math-academy-id "q-82985" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+3y$ and $v=x+2y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x+2y}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x+3y\\le 2,\\,2\\le x+2y\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=x+2y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x+2y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |-1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
